<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>Objects on STEP Batch 12</title><link>https://step-12.github.io/tags/objects/</link><description>Recent content in Objects on STEP Batch 12</description><generator>Hugo</generator><language>en_US</language><lastBuildDate>Sat, 10 Oct 2026 20:46:13 +0530</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://step-12.github.io/tags/objects/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><title>Iterating over Objects</title><link>https://step-12.github.io/posts/intro_js/object_api/</link><pubDate>Sat, 10 Oct 2026 20:44:00 +0530</pubDate><guid>https://step-12.github.io/posts/intro_js/object_api/</guid><description>&lt;h2 id="object-and-iteration-api"&gt;Object and Iteration API&lt;a class="anchor" href="#object-and-iteration-api"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;We have learned about arrays, objects, and loops.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;We know how to describe a thing using an object. We know how to keep a collection of values in an array. And we know how to use loops when we want to repeat an operation.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;JavaScript gives us a few more tools to work with these things.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;In this post, we will learn about:&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;ul&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;&lt;code&gt;Object.keys&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;&lt;code&gt;Object.values&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;&lt;code&gt;Object.entries&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;&lt;code&gt;for...of&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;&lt;code&gt;for...in&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;/ul&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;Nothing complicated. Just a few useful tools to add to our toolbox.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Objects in Raylib</title><link>https://step-12.github.io/posts/raylib/raylib_objects/</link><pubDate>Wed, 30 Sep 2026 08:30:00 +0530</pubDate><guid>https://step-12.github.io/posts/raylib/raylib_objects/</guid><description>&lt;h1 id="objects-in-raylib"&gt;Objects in Raylib&lt;a class="anchor" href="#objects-in-raylib"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;We have learned about objects in JavaScript.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;So far, we have mostly looked at objects as a way of putting related information together:&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;div class="highlight"&gt;&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma"&gt;&lt;code class="language-js" data-lang="js"&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;&lt;span class="kr"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;student&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&#10;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;&amp;#34;Ravi&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&#10;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;age&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;20&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&#10;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;The useful thing about this is not the curly braces. The useful thing is that we can give a shape to something we are thinking about.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;Raylib has many things that naturally have more than one piece of information.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;A colour has several components.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Objects part 2</title><link>https://step-12.github.io/posts/intro_js/objects_2/</link><pubDate>Mon, 28 Sep 2026 15:30:00 +0530</pubDate><guid>https://step-12.github.io/posts/intro_js/objects_2/</guid><description>&lt;h4 id="visualise-it"&gt;Visualise it&lt;a class="anchor" href="#visualise-it"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h4&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;At this point, it can be useful to stop reading and experiment with a &lt;a href="https://pythontutor.com/javascript.html#mode=edit"&gt;visualiser&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;Try entering small examples such as:&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;div class="highlight"&gt;&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma"&gt;&lt;code class="language-js" data-lang="js"&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;&lt;span class="kr"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&#10;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;x&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;10&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&#10;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;&#10;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;&#10;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;&lt;span class="kr"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;b&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;&#10;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;&#10;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;b&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;x&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;20&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;Watch what happens when &lt;code&gt;a&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;b&lt;/code&gt; refer to the same object.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;Then try:&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;div class="highlight"&gt;&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma"&gt;&lt;code class="language-js" data-lang="js"&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;let&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&#10;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;x&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;10&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&#10;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;&#10;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;&#10;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;let&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;b&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;&#10;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;&#10;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;b&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&#10;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;x&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;20&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&#10;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;Pay attention to the difference.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;The visualiser is not a substitute for understanding the code.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;Use it to test your mental model.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Objects</title><link>https://step-12.github.io/posts/intro_js/objects/</link><pubDate>Mon, 28 Sep 2026 14:30:00 +0530</pubDate><guid>https://step-12.github.io/posts/intro_js/objects/</guid><description>&lt;h2 id="objects"&gt;Objects&lt;a class="anchor" href="#objects"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;You have now written enough JavaScript to run into an interesting problem.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;You have variables.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;You have functions.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;You have started writing programs that are a little larger than a few lines.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;And now you may have noticed something.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;Sometimes, several values belong together.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;Suppose we want to describe a person.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;We might write:&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;div class="highlight"&gt;&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma"&gt;&lt;code class="language-js" data-lang="js"&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;&lt;span class="kr"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;&amp;#34;Asha&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;&#10;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;&lt;span class="kr"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;age&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;20&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;&#10;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;&lt;span class="kr"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;city&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;&amp;#34;Mysuru&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;There is nothing wrong with this.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;But look at those three variables.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;They all describe the same thing.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item></channel></rss>